    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Timur rose from a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barlas">Barlas</a> noble to master of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> by about 1370 and spent the next 35 years at war, defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</li><li>His campaigns relied on terror: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> were massacred, and scholars estimate that his wars caused the deaths of millions of people.</li><li>He enriched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and patronised scholars and poets, but his empire fragmented soon after his death; his descendants included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Timur's nickname, Temur-i Lang, 'Timur the Lame', from injuries to his right leg and hand, is the origin of the Western name Tamerlane; a Soviet examination of his remains in 1941 confirmed the injuries.</p></aside>
    </section>

