    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1370</span><span class="rf-text">Timur defeats his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-husayn">Amir Husayn</a> and is proclaimed sovereign at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1383</span><span class="rf-text">Timur begins his conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> with the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1387</span><span class="rf-text">After a revolt, Timur orders the massacre of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1395</span><span class="rf-text">Timur defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek River</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1398</span><span class="rf-text">Timur invades northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and sacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1401</span><span class="rf-text">Timur's armies sack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1402</span><span class="rf-text">Timur defeats and captures the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1405</span><span class="rf-text">Timur dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otrar">Farab</a> on campaign against Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Timur was born near Kesh, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrisabz">Shahrisabz</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, some 80 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, in what was then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>. Later Timurid histories give his birth as 8 April 1336, but most sources from his lifetime point to the late 1320s, and several scholars suspect the later date was chosen to link him to the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a>, Abu Sa'id, who died in 1336. His name means 'iron' in Chagatai, his mother tongue.</p><p>He belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barlas">Barlas</a>, a Mongol tribe that had become Turkicised in many ways and whose members had served the Chinggisid rulers since the 13th century. Historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-jackson">Peter Jackson</a> considers his Mongol ancestry generally accepted; Timurid histories trace his father's line to a male-line ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, though no evidence from his lifetime makes the link directly. His father Taraghai is described as a minor noble, but Beatrice Forbes Manz argues that Timur may later have understated his father's standing. The origins of his mother, Tekina Khatun, are unclear and the sources disagree.</p><p>As a youth he led a small band raiding travellers for animals. Around 1363, it is believed, he was shot by two arrows, in the right leg and the right hand, losing two fingers, either while stealing a sheep or, as some believe, while serving as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>. The injuries disabled him for life and gave him the Persian nickname Temur-i Lang, 'Timur the Lame', the source of 'Tamerlane'.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By about 1360 Timur had become a military leader of Turkic tribesmen. He allied himself with the amir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazaghan">Qazaghan</a> and led a thousand horsemen into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. After Qazaghan's murder, the eastern Chagatai khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlugh-timur">Tughlugh Timur</a> invaded; Timur, sent to negotiate, joined him instead and was rewarded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>. Around this time his father died, on 12 March 1360 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zafarnama">Zafarnama</a>'s account, and Timur became chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barlas">Barlas</a>. When Tughlugh tried to set his son Ilyas Khoja over Transoxiana, Timur repelled the invasion with a smaller force.</p><p>He and his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-husayn">Amir Husayn</a>, at first fellow fugitives, became rivals. Timur won followers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> among merchants, tribesmen, clergy and farmers by sharing his belongings, while Husayn alienated them with heavy taxes. Around 1370 Husayn surrendered and was later assassinated, and Timur was proclaimed sovereign at Balkh. He married Husayn's wife Saray Mulk Khanum, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>.</p><p>By Mongol tradition only a descendant of Genghis Khan could be khan, so Timur ruled as amir in the name of a puppet khan, Suyurghatmish, and took the title güregen, 'royal son-in-law'. He could not be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> either, and instead styled himself Sahib Qiran, 'Lord of the Conjunction', a title that A. Azfar Moin calls messianic, and presented himself as a spiritual descendant of Ali.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Timur spent 35 years at war. From 1383 he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, then divided among several dynasties after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>'s collapse, destroying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> when it would not surrender, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> in 1386 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> in 1393, when the Muzaffarids were annexed. In August 1393 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> by surprise after a march of eight days from Shiraz. He fought his former protégé <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, leading over 100,000 men deep into the steppe to win at the Kondurcha River in 1391 and defeating him finally at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek River</a> in 1395. His armies destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, and the Golden Horde never recovered.</p><p>In 1398 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> with 90,000 men into the declining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. On 17 December he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Nasir-ud-Din Mahmud Shah Tughluq before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, sending burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> against the sultan's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, and sacked one of the richest cities in the world.</p><p>From 1399 he fought the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and took Baghdad again in June 1401. On 20 July 1402 he defeated and captured Bayezid at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a>, opening the twelve-year Ottoman Interregnum, and in December took Smyrna from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitallers</a>. He ruled his core lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> through a centralised administration that joined Turko-Mongol traditions to a Persian bureaucracy, and brought scholars and artisans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Timur&#8221; cites where it covers Legitimization of Timur's rule, Conquest of Persia, Campaign against the Delhi Sultanate, Capture of Delhi (1398):</p><ul><li>Charles Melville, The Timurid Century: The Idea of Iran, Volume IX (Bloomsbury Publishing, 2020)</li><li>Beatrice Forbes Manz, The Rise and Rule of Tamerlane (Cambridge University Press, 1999)</li><li>Guy Le Strange, The Lands of the Eastern Caliphate: Mesopotamia, Persia, and Central Asia, from the Moslem Conquest to the Time of Timur (University Press, 1905)</li><li>Timur, The Mulfuzat Timury, Or, Autobiographical Memoirs of the Moghul Emperor Timur: Written in the Jagtay Turky Language (Cambridge University Press, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Timur's wars were waged with deliberate terror. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> revolted against his taxes in 1387 he ordered its people massacred; the death toll is reckoned at between 100,000 and 200,000, and an eyewitness counted more than 28 towers of about 1,500 heads each. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> was left in ruins, and prisoners at Isfizar were cemented alive into the walls. Before the battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> he had some 100,000 captive slaves killed for fear they would revolt; the city was sacked, its people enslaved and, after an uprising, massacred, and Delhi did not recover for almost a century. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1401, 20,000 people were killed, every soldier being ordered to bring back two severed heads. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> more than 60,000 people were taken as slaves. Scholars estimate that his campaigns caused the deaths of millions; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, which repeatedly rose against him, suffered most.</p><p>His main enemies were Muslim states, whose rulers and theologians accused him of unbelief. Ibn Arabshah wrote that he preferred the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and that many theologians declared him an unbeliever; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> called his army 'worse than the infidels', and Russian chronicles portrayed him as an evil pagan. He attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismailis</a> of Anjudan in 1393. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> historian Şihabetdin Märcani quoted the judgement that Timur did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> many times more harm than Genghis Khan or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>.</p><p>The sources are divided: William Jones contrasted the Timurid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zafarnama">Zafarnamas</a>' 'benevolent and illustrious prince' with Arabshah's 'deformed and impious' tyrant.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, Bayezid died in captivity, and his son Mehmed Çelebi acted as Timur's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, minting coins with Timur's name beside his own. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1402, Timur sent his grandson Abu Bakr to retake it. He then passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, where he gave captives to Ali Safavi, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safaviyya">Safaviyya</a>, and returned by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, where he spent nine months celebrating and preparing to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In Samarkand he received the Castilian embassy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruy-gonzalez-de-clavijo">Ruy González de Clavijo</a> in 1403–1406, and he corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a>.</p><p>He had refused the Ming emperors' claims to treat him as a subject, detaining their envoys. In December 1404, though he usually fought in spring, he set out on a winter campaign against Ming China. He fell ill in camp beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otrar">Farab</a> on 17 or 18 February 1405, before reaching the Chinese border. His body was embalmed and taken to Samarkand, where it lies in the Gur-e-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a>.</p><p>He had outlived both his chosen heirs: his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>, who died in 1376, and his grandson Muhammad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, who died of battle wounds in 1403. Only on his deathbed did he name Muhammad Sultan's younger brother Pir Muhammad, who could not win enough support. A bitter civil war followed among his descendants until his youngest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a>, took the throne in 1409.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Timur&#8221; cites where it covers Death:</p><ul><li>Shih-Shan Henry Tsai, Perpetual Happiness: the Ming Emperor Yongle (University of Washington Press, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Timur's legacy is mixed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> flourished under him and he keeps a positive image there, while he is vilified by many in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where some of his greatest atrocities took place. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, who met him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1401, praised him for uniting much of the Muslim world. Beatrice Forbes Manz sees his career as the last manifestation of the military power of the nomads, and his empire came before and made possible the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> empires of the 16th and 17th centuries. Beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> he plundered and neglected the lands he conquered, which may have helped his empire break up after his death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> later almost re-enacted his campaigns.</p><p>His reign led to the Timurid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Persian became the main language of administration and literature across his lands, while a literary Chagatai Turkic came into use beside it. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> ruled Central Asia as an astronomer-sultan, and his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. His campaigns also virtually exterminated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>, which was afterwards confined largely to a small area now called the Assyrian Triangle, and his wars hastened the breakup of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, which let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> rise.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> he is called the 'Founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> nation', and his monument in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> stands where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>'s statue once did. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> he became a figure of drama and opera, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-marlowe">Christopher Marlowe</a>'s Tamburlaine to Handel's Tamerlano. The supposed autobiography, the Malfuzat-i Timuri, is almost certainly a 17th-century fabrication.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Timur&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy, Exhumation and alleged curse, In the arts:</p><ul><li>Beatrice Forbes Manz, The Rise and Rule of Tamerlane (Cambridge University Press, 1989)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Timur, or Tamerlane, rose from a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barlas">Barlas</a> noble to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> between about 1370 and 1405. His conquests killed millions and wrecked cities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> flourished and his heirs led the Timurid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</em></p>

